<rml>
<head>
	<title>Login</title>
	<link type="text/rcss" href="base.rcss"/>
	<link type="text/rcss" href="login.rcss"/>
</head>
<body>
	<!-- Modern theme's own copy of the login panel. Per-theme file (UI::RmlBridge::LoadThemedDocument)
	     because modern's decorative layering is genuinely modern-specific structure, not just style --
	     the shared ../../login.rml is legacy's own theme-neutral copy and carries none of these
	     classes (a "shared" file must not bake in one theme's vocabulary). Keep this file's
	     ids/data-model/event-callback names in sync with
	     ../../login.rml -- LoginWin.cpp binds against both by the same names.

	     Reference construction for how a modern-theme dialog is built -- other windows (e.g.
	     sys_menu.rml) reuse base.rcss's shared primitives (.modern-shell-edge/.modern-content-well/
	     .modern-footer-band/.modern-title-plate/.modern-control-well/.modern-header-rail/
	     .modern-joint) rather than duplicating this structure; .modern-header-rail/.modern-joint
	     specifically are NOT used by Login, see the note below. Depth comes from real box-shadow
	     directly on window/header/body/hero-plate (the vendored SDL_GPU renderer's PR-989
	     integration implements it -- see base.rcss's own header comment for the full source trace),
	     so the shell reads as outer iron edge -> window surface -> inner groove, without extra
	     shadow-plane divs. The hero-plate's diamond studs use a real `transform: rotate(45deg)`
	     square -- SetTransform is genuinely wired into this renderer's vertex stage (verified
	     against source). #panel uses plain .modern-frame (NOT the -crimson variant -- crimson
	     belongs to the hero banner only, the outer window shell is neutral blackened iron), and
	     login.rcss overrides its background/decorator/border directly to a single uniform border
	     tone + real box-shadow, since box-shadow now carries all of the surface's depth. -->
	<div id="panel" class="modern-frame" data-model="login">
		<!-- Exterior blackened-metal silhouette: outer iron edge, -3dp. -->
		<div class="modern-shell-edge login-shell-edge"></div>

		<!-- Inner groove, ~4dp inward. -->
		<div class="login-groove"></div>

		<!-- The reference defines TWO separate, mutually-exclusive header treatments:
		     .window__header (metal, ONE center rivet via ::before) and .hero-banner (crimson, TWO
		     side rivets via ::before/::after) -- different windows use one or the other, never
		     both nested together. Login's header IS the hero-banner directly (it's a "hero"
		     identity display -- which server you're connecting to), spanning the full header
		     footprint itself; there is no separate iron rail layer or center-rivet joint
		     underneath it. Crimson lives ONLY on this banner -- the outer window shell (#panel,
		     plain .modern-frame, no -crimson variant) is neutral blackened iron, and there is no
		     ambient glow washing crimson/warm light onto that shell (the reference's own warm
		     highlight is just the banner's own internal top gradient layer, not a separate glow
		     element). -->
		<div class="modern-title-plate login-hero-banner">
			<!-- Diamonds INSIDE the plate, left/right:8dp from its own edges -- the hero-banner's
			     own ornament set, matching the reference exactly. -->
			<div class="login-hero-cap login-hero-cap-left"></div>
			<div class="login-hero-cap login-hero-cap-right"></div>
			<span class="label label-server">{{server_name}}</span>
		</div>

		<!-- Real recessed body: controls are structurally inside the well. -->
		<div class="modern-content-well login-content">
			<span class="label label-account">{{account_label}}</span>
			<span class="label label-password">{{password_label}}</span>

			<div class="login-input modern-control-well login-input-account"></div>
			<div class="login-input modern-control-well login-input-password"></div>

			<div class="checkbox-row checkbox-remember-me" data-event-click="login_toggle_remember_me">
				<div class="checkbox-box" data-class-checked="remember_me_checked">
					<div class="checkbox-tick"></div>
				</div>
				<span class="checkbox-label">{{remember_me_label}}</span>
			</div>

			<div class="checkbox-row checkbox-save-password" data-event-click="login_toggle_save_password">
				<div class="checkbox-box" data-class-checked="save_password_checked">
					<div class="checkbox-tick"></div>
				</div>
				<span class="checkbox-label">{{save_password_label}}</span>
			</div>
		</div>

		<!-- Footer: a structural container only -- see base.rcss's own .modern-footer-band comment.
		     One plain border-top (login.rcss's .login-footer) carries the entire body-to-footer
		     separation now, matching the reference's own `.window__footer{border-top:1px solid
		     #24251f;background:none}` exactly -- the previous two-div shadow+shine pair was more
		     decorative than the reference. -->
		<div class="modern-footer-band login-footer">
			<div class="btn btn-ok login-btn login-btn-ok" data-event-click="login_ok_click">
				<span class="login-btn-label">{{ok_label}}</span>
			</div>
			<div class="btn btn-cancel login-btn login-btn-cancel" data-event-click="login_cancel_click">
				<span class="login-btn-label">{{cancel_label}}</span>
			</div>
		</div>

		<span class="trust-warning">{{trust_warning}}</span>
	</div>
</body>
</rml>
